1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,100
Cancer can come back.

2
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:02,520
So can an anal fistula.

3
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:04,100
Even with all the progress in medicine,

4
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:06,440
there is no promise that
either is gone forever.

5
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:09,270
A fistula is like having a
second opening near your anus.

6
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:12,320
It can leak, bleed,
and cause serious pain.

7
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,460
I had one, and I went
through two surgeries.

8
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:16,440
Recovery did not end after surgery.

9
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:19,100
I still had medicines to
remember, symptoms to track,

10
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,720
wounds to monitor, and
questions for my doctor.

11
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:24,240
That is why I built Fistula Tracker.

12
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,490
When you first open the app, the
onboarding introduces my story

13
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:29,110
and explains why I created it.

14
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:32,170
It also asks a few questions
about your condition and recovery.

15
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:33,420
Your answers are saved locally

16
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,030
and used to adapt the app's
experience to your needs.

17
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:39,300
After onboarding, the home screen gives
you a simple view of your recovery

18
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:41,250
and shows what needs your attention today.

19
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:43,680
Daily check-ins guide you
through a few short questions.

20
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,450
You can record pain, discharge,
bowel movements, and other changes.

21
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:49,380
Your previous check-ins are saved by date,

22
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,250
helping you understand how your
recovery has changed over time.

23
00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:54,600
The medicine section helps
you organize what you take,

24
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:58,250
add instructions, and create
reminders for important doses.

25
00:00:58,270 --> 00:01:00,340
You can also create
simple recovery routines

26
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,150
and keep your daily tasks together.

27
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:05,100
The journal gives you a private
place to save thoughts, questions,

28
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,190
and anything you want to remember
for your next appointment.

29
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,140
You can save dated recovery photos too.

30
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:14,010
They stay organized alongside
the rest of your recovery history

31
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:16,520
instead of getting lost
in your normal gallery.

32
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:19,210
Fistula Tracker requires no account.

33
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:22,680
Your onboarding answers, check-ins,
medicines, journal entries, and photos

34
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:24,390
stay locally on your device.

35
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:26,730
The app does not diagnose
you, recommend treatment,

36
00:01:26,750 --> 00:01:28,640
or replace professional medical care.

37
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:31,170
It simply helps you
organize your own recovery.

38
00:01:31,190 --> 00:01:32,910
Every recovery feature is free.

39
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:36,010
There are no advertisements,
subscriptions, or locked care tools.

40
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:38,920
RevenueCat powers one optional
Lifetime Supporter purchase

41
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,460
for people who want to
support the project.

42
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:45,020
Whether you purchase it or not, every
recovery tool remains available.

43
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,700
Care is never the paywall.

44
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,600
I built Fistula Tracker because
it is the app I wish I had

45
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:50,890
during my own recovery.

46
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:52,720
I hope you never need it.

47
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:54,990
But if you do, I hope it makes recovery…

48
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:58,560
more organized, more private,
and a little less lonely.
